#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:3
msgid "Writing tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:5
msgid ""
"There are two test surfaces in this project, with different owners and "
"different purposes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:8
msgid "Which of them run at every commit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:10
msgid ""
"Every suite here is worth running, and not every suite is worth running "
"*every time*. The line is drawn by cost, and :ref:`fast-and-slow` is the "
"whole argument: a gate that costs a minute is a gate people commit "
"around, and a check that gets skipped has no value at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:20
msgid ""
"**Deferring a suite does not hide it.** Each one records its outcome and "
"the commit it ran at, so ``make ci-check`` prints a suite nobody ran here"
" as ``NOT RUN`` and one that last ran elsewhere as ``STALE`` — neither of"
" which reads as a pass. On a production branch each of those refuses the "
"commit, and the hook runs ``make ci-full`` first so that nothing is left "
"unknown. The table of suites, and what each verdict means, is in "
":doc:`../devops/index`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:26
msgid ""
"If you are about to move a test across the line, read the tip under *What"
" marks a test slow* in :doc:`../devops/index` first. Twice in this "
"repository the slow thing turned out not to be the test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:30
msgid "The engine's own tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:32
msgid ""
"The bulk of the coverage is in the Rust crate, as unit tests inside the "
"module they exercise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:41
msgid ""
"The unit tests are in the gate because they are the ones that answer a "
"keystroke: 0.7 s, no fixture files, no whole-document flow. The "
"integration tests are what would notice a regression across the whole "
"flow and cost four times as much between them, so they are run when you "
"ask — which the verdict table then reminds you to do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:46
msgid ""
"How much of it is covered is a *gated* figure, not a matter of taste: "
"``make coverage-rust`` and ``make coverage-python`` measure it and ``make"
" ci-check`` compares it against the minimum this repository sets for "
"itself. Both are slow — one recompiles the crate instrumented, the other "
"re-runs both Python suites under ``coverage.py`` — so neither is in the "
"commit gate, and both are read from the last full run. "
":doc:`../devops/index` says where those minima live and what happens when"
" one is missed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:52
msgid "Two conventions are not optional:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:54
msgid ""
"**group by topic.** Tests live in nested modules inside ``mod tests``, "
"one module per behaviour under test, never a flat list of ``#[test]`` "
"functions. A file with two hundred flat tests is a file nobody can "
"navigate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:57
msgid ""
"**exhaust, do not sample.** Cover the branches, including the ones that "
"only fail, and stress the edges — a parser is expected to survive any "
"input without panicking, and that is a test, not a hope."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:61
msgid ""
"A handful of tests reach Python, because two modules genuinely do: "
"loading a document, and running an author's pipe. Everything else stays "
"native, which is what keeps the suite fast and deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:66
msgid "A format's tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:68
msgid ""
"Tests for a format live in its **formats repository**, not here, and are "
"run with ``freeports-dev``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:75
msgid "Each format under ``tests/formats/<FORMAT>/`` has three things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:77
msgid "``report.pdf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:78
msgid "the document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:80
msgid "``pages/<page class>/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:81
msgid ""
"per-page fixtures — ``<page>-pdf_blks.json``, ``<page>-txt_blks.json``, "
"``<page>-results.json``, and a ``filter_data.json`` per page class. They "
"pin one page at a time, which is what tells you *which segment* broke "
"rather than that the document did. Generate them with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:89
msgid "They are JSON on purpose: a regression should be visible in a diff."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:91
msgid "``out/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:92
msgid ""
"the expected output of the whole document. This is the repository's "
"specification, not a snapshot: if a run diverges from it, the engine "
"changed, and the divergence is the finding. Regenerating one of these "
"files is a deliberate act with a reason attached — and because grants are"
" made against the content of exactly these files, regenerating one "
"invalidates the grant on it. See :doc:`../../overview/trust`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:98
msgid ""
"The whole-document tests are slower than the per-page ones by a wide "
"margin — a minute and a half against five seconds, in "
"``analysis_finance_reports_formats``, because each one is a full "
"extraction run. So they are two suites, gated the way the engine's are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:108
msgid ""
"in that repository, or from anywhere against a repository that is not "
"yours:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/tests.rst:116
msgid ""
"The targets and the flags are the same three selections — a format "
"repository's ``Makefile`` is the engine's, with the axes it has no use "
"for taken out. Run the per-page ones constantly: that is the loop format "
"development actually happens in, and it is the one that tells you *which "
"segment* broke rather than that the document did. That repository's "
"commit hook runs them at every commit and the whole-document ones on a "
"production branch, and ``make ci-check`` says which of the two describes "
"the commit in front of you."
msgstr ""

